# A short story for the Aside engine.
# Fifth register. Night Shift is a demo, Overtime is horror and performance, The Lamp Room is warm and about
# tending, The Discrepancy is quiet and wry, The Water Line is elegiac and practical. All five of those are ONE
# person alone with a task.
#
# This one is TWO people, and that is the whole point of it: a brother and a sister clearing out their mother's
# house in a weekend, talking to each other instead of to the reader. Warm, funny where it can be, and about the
# difference between what a family keeps and what it says. Six scenes, three endings, one sitting.
#
# The thing it is actually about: a house with two of everything is a house where two people disagreed and neither
# of them ever threw anything away.

title: Two of Everything
author: Roxanne
start: kitchen

== kitchen ==
show nell neutral at left
bg kitchen_boxes
music fan_hum
Nell gets there first, which she will mention, and puts the kettle on, which she will also mention.

There are boxes in the kitchen. There were boxes in the kitchen when they were children and there are boxes in
the kitchen now, and the difference is that these ones have her mother's handwriting on them, and say things
like: spoons (good).

Tom arrives at eleven with a van and an apology. The apology is for being late. The van is for the piano.

* "There's tea." -> tea
* "You brought the van." -> van

== tea ==
show nell neutral at left
show tom neutral at right
He takes the tea. He stands in the doorway of the kitchen he grew up in and does not sit down, because every
chair has something on it.

"She labelled the spoons," he says.

"She labelled everything. There are two of everything, Tom. Two toasters. Two of the good scissors."

"You know why."

"I know why."

* "Say it anyway." -> say_it
* "Don't. Let's start." -> loft

== van ==
show nell neutral at left
show tom neutral at right
"I brought the van," he says. "For the piano."

"You're not taking the piano."

"I'm not taking the piano. I'm taking the opportunity to have brought a van."

Nell laughs, which surprises both of them, and then they stand there for a moment being pleased about it. Two
toasters. Two sets of the good scissors. Their mother, who never threw anything away because throwing it away
would have meant admitting whose it was.

* "Say the thing about the toasters." -> say_it
* "Come on. The loft first." -> loft

== say_it ==
show nell neutral at left
show tom neutral at right
"Dad's," Tom says. "It was all Dad's, and she couldn't throw it out, and she couldn't use it either, so it lived
in a cupboard for twenty years being his."

"That's not a reason to keep two toasters."

"It's a reason they came in twos."

Nell puts her tea down on a box marked *cups (everyday)* and looks at the room the way she looks at a problem at
work, which is the only way she has ever looked at anything.

"Right," she says. "Loft."

* Go up to the loft. -> loft

== loft ==
show nell neutral at left
show tom neutral at right
bg loft_light
The loft is hot and full of light and the sort of dust that has opinions.

There are two suitcases. Matching. His and hers, and hers has a label on it with a destination on it that is not
this house, and the label is forty years old and the handwriting on it is her mother's, young.

Tom sits down on the floor of the loft, which he has not done since he was nine.

"She nearly went," he says. "She had the case packed and a label written and she stayed, and then we got two of
everything."

* "She chose us." -> chose
* "We don't know that. She might have unpacked it a hundred times." -> unpacked

== chose ==
show nell neutral at left
show tom neutral at right
"That's what I tell myself," Tom says.

"That's what I tell myself too," Nell says. "Which is why it's true."

They carry the suitcases down together and put them by the van, and it takes the rest of the day to do the loft,
and they do not talk about the label again, which is a kind of agreement.

* At the end of the day. -> table

== unpacked ==
show nell neutral at left
show tom neutral at right
"That is genuinely the worst thing you have ever said to me in a loft," Tom says.

"Probably."

They laugh, and it is not entirely a joke, and Nell sits down on the floor next to him in the dust in her good
trousers, which for Nell is close to a confession.

"If she unpacked it a hundred times," she says, "she packed it a hundred times too."

* At the end of the day. -> table

== table ==
show nell neutral at left
show tom neutral at right
bg table_evening
By six the house is a house. It is astonishing how quickly a house stops being somebody's.

The van is full. There are two boxes on the kitchen table that are not going in it, and both of them know which
two, and neither of them has moved them.

The label on the case and the second set of scissors. One is going to Tom's and one is going to Nell's, and
whoever picks first gives the other one away, and they are both standing at the table being extremely casual
about it.

* "You take the case. You've got the room." -> ending_case
* "I'll take the scissors. You had them last." -> ending_scissors
* "Leave them both on the table and lock up." -> ending_table

== ending_case ==
show nell neutral at center
bg table_evening
He takes the case. She does not say anything about it, and she takes nothing at all, which he notices and does not
mention, and which will be the thing she is angry about in about four years.

Driving away, Nell thinks: the scissors were the better thing to want.

She is still thinking it at the first roundabout, and at the second, and then she stops, because she has just
understood that he took the case for the same reason she wanted the scissors, which is that neither of them
wanted the thing itself. They wanted to be the one who was given it.

-> END

== ending_scissors ==
show nell neutral at left
show tom neutral at right
bg front_step
She takes the scissors, and she does not even like the scissors, and he knows she does not like the scissors.

"You didn't want those," he says, on the step.

"I wanted them more than you wanted the case."

He looks at her. He is the younger one and he has always been able to tell, and she watches him decide not to
say the true thing, which is that the case was never going to be his and the scissors were never going to be
hers and their mother had forty years to say what she meant and she labelled the spoons instead.

"Right," he says. "See you at Christmas."

"See you at Christmas."

-> END

== ending_table ==
bg table_evening
They put both boxes back on the table with the same hands at the same time, which takes some doing.

Tom locks up. Nell waits in the van. Neither of them says the obvious thing, which is that the next person
through that door will be a stranger with a clipboard, and the stranger will take the case and the scissors and
everything else on the table, and will not know what any of it was for.

What they have done instead is leave the house exactly as their mother kept it: two of everything, neither one
chosen, nobody admitting whose it was.

It is a terrible way to end and it is also, and both of them know this, the only one that is actually about her.

-> END
